<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n4008" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">assectātĭo</span></b>  (<b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ads-</span></b> ), ōnis,
			f.
		assector. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>An</i> (<i>assiduous</i>, <i>respectful</i>) <i>attendance</i> (as
			that of clients, etc.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">in petitionibus opera atque adsectatio,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic. Mur. 34</span></i>
			: so <i><span class="en">Q. Cic. Petit. Cons.
			9</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>Observation</i>, <i>study</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">magna caeli adsectatio,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					2, 20, 18, § 82</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
